{0 Widgets and themes}

This page shows the widgets of {!Console} that the README leaves out, and
how the themes draw them. Each example prints without colour, as it would
on a pipe, so its output is exactly the text a terminal shows.

{1:text Styled text}

A {{!Console.View}view} is text in one or more styles. Parts of it can be
links, which a terminal that supports them opens on a click. Without colour
a link prints as its label:

{@ocaml[
# let docs =
    Console.View.(
      styled Console.Style.bold (text "Read ")
      ++ link ~uri:"https://ocaml.org/docs"
           (styled Console.Style.underline (text "the manual")));;
val docs : Console.View.t = <abstr>
# Fmt.pr "%a@." Console.View.pp docs;;
Read the manual
- : unit = ()
]}

A role gives a view one of the theme's colours, chosen only when the view is
printed:

{ul
{- {!Console.View.role} styles any view, as [View.(role `Succeeded (text "passed"))]
   does, a link for example.}
{- The roles are [`Accent], [`Succeeded], [`Warning], [`Failed], [`Muted] and
   [`Header] ({!Console.Theme.type-role}).}}

{!Console.View.gradient} grades text through the colours it is given, or
through the theme's palette, one grapheme cluster at a time. Without colour
it is its text:

{@ocaml[
# Fmt.pr "%a@." Console.View.pp
    (Console.View.gradient ~colors:Console.Color.[ red; blue ]
       (Console.View.text "console"));;
console
- : unit = ()
]}

{1:tables Table borders and rules}

{!Console.Border.simple} draws no frame, only a rule under the header:

{@ocaml[
# print_endline
    (Console.View.to_string (Console.Table.view ~border:Console.Border.simple
     [
      Console.Table.column (Console.View.text "Name");
      Console.Table.column ~align:`Right (Console.View.text "Size");
     ]
     [
      [ Console.View.text "README.md"; Console.View.text "2.4K" ];
      [ Console.View.text "src/"; Console.View.text "4.0K" ];
     ]));;
Name       Size
───────────────
README.md  2.4K
src/       4.0K
- : unit = ()
]}

[~rules:true] draws a rule between the rows, which helps the eye when cells
hold several lines:

{@ocaml[
# print_endline
    (Console.View.to_string (Console.Table.view ~rules:true
     [
      Console.Table.column (Console.View.text "Step");
      Console.Table.column (Console.View.text "Status");
     ]
     [
      [ Console.View.text "fetch"; Console.View.text "done" ];
      [ Console.View.text "compile"; Console.View.text "2 warnings\n1 error" ];
     ]));;
╭─────────┬────────────╮
│ Step    │ Status     │
├─────────┼────────────┤
│ fetch   │ done       │
├─────────┼────────────┤
│ compile │ 2 warnings │
│         │ 1 error    │
╰─────────┴────────────╯
- : unit = ()
]}

{1:nesting Widgets side by side}

Every widget is a view, laid out only when it is printed, in the room it
is given. {!Console.View.hcat} puts views side by side, each at its
natural width, or at the width {!Console.View.sized} gives it, and in its
own colours. The result is one more view, which can go in a cell, a panel
or a display's view. {!Console.View.vcat} puts views one under the other:

{@ocaml[
# let tree =
    Console.Tree.v (Console.View.text "src")
      [ Console.Tree.v (Console.View.text "lib") [] ]
  and panel = Console.Panel.view (Console.View.text "2 files") in
  Fmt.pr "%a@." Console.View.pp
    (Console.View.hcat
       [ Console.Tree.view tree; panel ]);;
src      ╭─────────╮
└── lib  │ 2 files │
         ╰─────────╯
- : unit = ()
]}

{1:themes Themes}

A {{!Console.Theme}theme} holds the choices the widgets share:

{ul
{- the main colour, and the colours of a success, a warning and a failure;}
{- a muted colour for frames and annotations, and the style of headers;}
{- the marks that say how a task ended;}
{- the frame of tables and panels, and how it moves;}
{- the lines of a tree, the glyphs of a bar and the spinner.}}

A formatter has one theme, which {!Console.Theme.set} chooses much as
[Fmt.set_style_renderer] chooses its colours, and every widget printed on
that formatter is drawn in its theme.

{2:choosing Choosing a theme}

{ul
{- {!Console.Theme.default} is drawn in the terminal's own named colours,
   with faint text for what is muted, so it reads on whatever background
   the terminal's user chose. Keep it when nothing is known of the
   terminal.}
{- A theme named after a colour scheme, such as {!Console.Theme.dracula},
   is for a terminal that uses that scheme. Its colours are chosen for the
   scheme's background, which console does not paint, so some of them may
   be hard to read on another one.}
{- On a terminal of 16 colours, as [TERM=xterm] announces without a
   [COLORTERM], every theme is drawn in the terminal's named colours. Their
   contrast is that of the terminal's own scheme.}
{- Every theme falls back to ASCII on a formatter that is not UTF-8.
   {!Console.Theme.ascii} always draws in ASCII.}}

{!Console.Theme.all} lists the themes under their names, the values of a
[--theme] option:

{@ocaml[
# List.map fst Console.Theme.all;;
- : string list =
["default"; "ascii"; "dos"; "matrix"; "rainbow"; "dracula"; "nord";
 "solarized-dark"; "solarized-light"; "gruvbox-dark"; "gruvbox-light";
 "catppuccin-latte"; "catppuccin-mocha"; "tokyo-night"; "one-dark";
 "monokai"; "github-dark"; "github-light"]
]}

{2:motion Frames that move}

In a theme with {{!Console.Theme.type-motion}motion}, the frame of a table
or a panel in a {!Console.Display}'s view moves, by the time since the
display started, while the display draws in colour:

{ul
{- {!Console.Theme.matrix} runs digital rain round it ([`Rain]);}
{- {!Console.Theme.rainbow} sweeps its palette round it ([`Sweep]);}
{- every theme named after a colour scheme sends a highlight of its main
   colour round it ([`Shimmer]);}
{- {!Console.Theme.default}, {!Console.Theme.ascii} and
   {!Console.Theme.dos} stand still.}}

Only the colours and the glyphs of straight lines change, and each glyph
keeps its width, so the columns stay aligned in every frame.

A frame stands still when it is printed with [Fmt], on a pipe or without
colour, and a rainbow frame then keeps its colours.

{!Console.Theme.still} takes all motion out of a theme, the spinner
included, for the users who ask for no animation:

{@ocaml[
let theme ~no_motion t = if no_motion then Console.Theme.still t else t
]}
